      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] ❌ **Changes Requested** — Please address the inline comments regarding extracting the loop limit constant and simplifying/expanding the unit test.\n\n### Review Summary\n* **Functionality:** ✅ Bounding the `do-while` loop in `handle_irqs()` prevents the high-priority RTS54xx driver thread from spinning indefinitely and starving lower-priority threads when an IRQ line stays asserted while ARA continues responding.\n* **Architecture \u0026 Component Fit:** ✅ Bounding the IRQ loop inside `handle_irqs()` in `zephyr/drivers/usbc/pdc_rts54xx.c` fits the driver state machine and thread model.\n* **Code Simplicity:** ❌ In `zephyr/test/pdc/src/rts54xx/rts54xx.c`, `stuck_ara_read_cb()` includes an unreachable `STUCK_IRQ_MAX_ARA_READS` (`50`) guard and `-EIO` branch that can be removed once `handle_irqs()` is bounded.\n* **Idiomaticity \u0026 Readability:** ❌ In `zephyr/drivers/usbc/pdc_rts54xx.c`, the loop limit `10` (`int limit \u003d 10;`) is an inline magic number and is duplicated in the test as `STUCK_IRQ_EXPECTED_LIMIT`; it should be extracted to a named `#define` constant consistent with `N_I2C_TRANSACTION_COUNT` and other driver retry limits.\n* **Security:** ✅ No security concerns identified.\n* **Testing:** ❌ `test_handle_irqs_limit` verifies the upper bound when the IRQ stays asserted, but should assert `read_count` before test cleanup and also verify the `any_irq_gpio_asserted()` termination path below the limit (e.g., `RTS54XX_MAX_IRQ_LOOP_COUNT - 1`) so both clauses of `while (any_irq_gpio_asserted() \u0026\u0026 --limit \u003e 0)` are covered.\n* **CL Size \u0026 Scope:** ✅ `Size: M (+55, -1)` (6 production lines, 49 test lines). Single, focused logical change.\n* **Commit Message:** ✅ Clear, concise prose explaining both the starvation root cause and the fix, with valid `BUG\u003d` and `TEST\u003d` footers.\n\n[🔗](http://go/tj/d23afc59-c1db-4742-8f70-262543e846f7)",
